    <dialog id="build-notes-dialog" class="sf-dialog sf-build-notes" aria-labelledby="build-notes-title" aria-describedby="build-notes-copy"><div><div class="sf-drawer-heading"><div><p class="sf-kicker">Product plan</p><h2 id="build-notes-title">How Set could scale.</h2></div></div><p id="build-notes-copy" class="sf-dialog-copy">The demo keeps the member, partner, and operations views in sync. A production service would enforce the same rules with accounts, permissions, and a transaction-backed schedule.</p><details class="sf-plan-section" open><summary>Accounts and permissions</summary><p>People use individual accounts. Partner roles belong to each studio; Set operations roles stay separate. Every change checks role and organization scope and records who changed what.</p></details><details class="sf-plan-section"><summary>Classes and bookings</summary><p>Recurring class templates create dated sessions. Each booking checks eligibility and capacity, while an append-only credit ledger records charges, refunds, and adjustments.</p></details><details class="sf-plan-section"><summary>Operations and reliability</summary><p>Member schedules, partner rosters, and support records read from the same session and booking history. Production checks would cover permissions, duplicate requests, and concurrent bookings.</p></details><div class="sf-dialog-actions"><button id="build-notes-close" class="sf-primary-action" type="button">Back to demo</button></div></div></dialog>
